WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.029
Robert E. Lee - today, the mere mention of
his name is enough to arouse passionate debate.

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:07.819
In his time, he was loved and respected by
both the Confederate Army and the Southern

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:08.819
people.

00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:13.030
Curiously, following the Civil War, this high
admiration carried over to include the people

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.830
of the North, and Lee become a cherished figure
for all Americans.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.500
During the war, when Abraham Lincoln looked
at a picture of Lee, he remarked that a man

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.030
with such a compassionate countenance had
to be a ‘good man.’

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.860
But the war to which he devoted his every
fiber broke him, if not in spirit, certainly

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.010
in body and he was only to outlive the conflict
by five years.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:42.060
In this week’s Biographics, we discover
the man who was Robert E. Lee.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:53.379
Early Life

00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:57.870
Robert E. Lee was born on January 19, 1807
at Stratford Hill Plantation in Westmoreland

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.100
County, Virginia.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.539
His father, a wealthy Virginia aristocrat,
had been a hero during the American Revolution.

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:07.400
A series of bad investments left ‘Light
Horse Harry’ penniless, and he fled Virginia

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.530
and his family, never to return.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.500
Lee was raised by his mother, a proud, educated
woman.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.050
She was to see to it that her son was not
deprived of a proper upbringing because he

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:21.750
was fatherless, and that he was well educated
in the arts, languages and philosophy.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.830
Young Robert secured an appointment to the
United States Military Academy at West Point

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:31.110
in 1825, and he graduated second in his class
to immediately begin his career in the United

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.520
States Army.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:42.920
A Military Career

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.910
Lee distinguished himself in the Mexican War,
where he served on the staff of a fellow Virginian,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.380
General Winfield Scott.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.770
As a captain, Lee was responsible for several
courageous personal reconnaissance missions,

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.850
which produced intelligence that led to American
victories.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.520
During this period, when the military had
no medals for bravery, officers were given

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.680
‘brevet promotions’ for brave deeds during
warfare.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.010
Captain Lee received three of these brevets
during the Mexican War: to major, lieutenant

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.830
colonel, and finally colonel.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:13.640
Scott described Lee as ‘the very best soldier
I ever saw in the field.’

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.180
Lee progressed through a series of military
assignments in the peacetime army and was

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.120
in command of the First Cavalry in Texas as
the Civil War was beginning.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.349
Lee got caught up early in the political turmoil
that was about to engulf the nation.

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:29.409
While he was on leave from his position in
Texas, John Brown attacked the armory at Harper’s

00:02:29.409 --> 00:02:33.049
Ferry, an event that clearly set the nation
on the road to war.

00:02:33.049 --> 00:02:37.129
President Buchanan called on Lee to take command
of a small Federal force that would be responsible

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:39.439
for bringing the situation under control.

00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:43.550
Lee and a future subordinate who was also
on leave, J.E.B Stuart, took command of a

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:47.450
small group of marines and captured the insurgents
with Brown.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:51.859
As the nation began to fracture, Lee - like
most of his contemporaries in the army - was

00:02:51.859 --> 00:02:55.889
placed in a difficult situation, as it became
obvious that the southern states planned to

00:02:55.889 --> 00:02:57.959
defend themselves with arms.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:02.359
General Winfield Scott, the aging patriarch
of the army, urged Lincoln to offer Lee command

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:03.359
of the army.

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:07.200
Learning of this offer, Lee struggled with
the definition of ‘duty’, a word that

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.620
had meant everything to him as an officer
in the United States army.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:17.879
A Fateful Decision

00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:25.919
On the April 19th, 1861 after Virginia had
quit the Union, Robert E. Lee faced the most

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:27.590
difficult decision of his life.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.110
It kept him up all night, pacing the wooden
floor of his Arlington mansion.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.629
Lee’s daughter, Mary Custis Lee, recalled
that, that night, Arlington was like a place

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:36.889
where a death had occurred.

00:03:36.889 --> 00:03:39.940
The death in question was the end of a brilliant
military career.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.999
Lee’s wife, Mary, later wrote . . .

00:03:41.999 --> 00:03:45.220
My husband has wept tears of blood over this
terrible war.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.050
But, as a man of honor, and as a Virginian,
he must follow the destiny of his state.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:54.450
It was the severest struggle of his life - to
resign a commission he had held for thirty

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.450
years.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.319
To accept the command would undoubtedly mean
fighting against his native Virginia and the

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:03.059
South, something he couldn't bring himself
to do.

00:04:03.059 --> 00:04:07.189
Resignation from the army was also a difficult
choice to make, and as he wrestled with his

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:11.299
decision, he was attended by the prospect
of bloodshed on either side.

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:15.650
Finally, he decided to resign his commission
in the army and attempt to avoid service with

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.209
either side in the forthcoming conflict.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.459
Lee himself wrote . . .

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.060
With all my devotion to the Union and the
feeling of loyalty and duty of an American

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.120
citizen, I have not been able to make up my
mind to raise my hand against my relatives,

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.630
my children, my home.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.240
I have therefore, resigned my commission in
the army.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.270
Except in the defense of my native state,
I hope to never draw my sword again.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.520
This statement was to prove to be prophetic.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:43.400
On Monday, April 22nd, 1861 Lee left his home
at Arlington, never to return.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.240
Within two weeks, Union troops occupied his
house.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.520
Mary and their four girls were vagabonds of
war.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.370
His three sons were soldiers in grey, while
Lee was in Richmond planning the defense of

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.370
Virginia.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.680
The general belief among politicians and militarists
in the South was that the war would be short.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.650
But Lee believed otherwise, prophetically
stating . . .

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:04.550
If it comes to a conflict of arms, the war
will last at least four years.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.629
Northern politicians do not appreciate the
determination and pluck of the South and Southern

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:13.639
politicians do not appreciate the numbers,
resources and patient perseverance of the

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:14.639
North.

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:16.310
Both sides forget that we are all Americans.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:21.699
I foresee that the country will have to pass
through a terrible ordeal, a necessary expiation

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:26.669
perhaps for our national sins.

00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:33.789
The War

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:36.940
During the early days of the war, it became
apparent to Lee that the South would fight

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:41.800
a defensive war, and that his beloved Virginia
would become the major battlefield of that

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:42.800
war.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.670
He accepted a commission as a commander of
Virginia’s troops and was soon commissioned

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:48.229
as a brigadier general in the Confederate
army.

00:05:48.229 --> 00:05:51.770
He remained in relatively obscure positions
during the first year of the war as he gained

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:56.370
his earliest Civil War experience in the mountainous
region of western Virginia.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:00.699
Here the poor weather, combined with the political
infighting between two of Virginia’s former

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:05.470
governors - now generals - resulted in disastrous
military failures for the Confederacy.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:09.720
Lee was attacked in Southern newspapers as
‘Evacuating Lee’ and ‘Granny Lee.’

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.349
His hair and his new beard turned gray during
the tension-filled mountain campaigns.

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:18.909
Lee’s next assignment was on the Carolina
sea coast, where he was responsible for building

00:06:18.909 --> 00:06:22.629
defenses to prevent large-scale Union army
attacks from the sea.

00:06:22.629 --> 00:06:27.720
He placed his strong defensive positions upstream,
where shallow rivers could not be easily navigated

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.020
by the Union navy.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:33.280
Lee’s ability as a military engineer was
proved by the fact that his defenses were

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:38.169
to last for much of the war, but he was nicknamed
‘King of Spades’ because of the construction

00:06:38.169 --> 00:06:39.599
work he managed.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:44.250
His detractors, however, were soon to realize
their mistake in misjudging this aggressive

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.250
officer.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.280
When his West Point classmate, Joseph E. Johnston,
was severely wounded at the Battle of Seven

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:53.969
Pines in June, 1862, Lee was placed in command
of what was soon to become the legendary Army

00:06:53.969 --> 00:06:55.400
of Northern Virginia.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:59.889
As Union General George MccLellan advanced
towards the new Confederate capital, Lee ordered

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:04.270
his men to begin digging defenses for Richmond
- giving new credence to one of his nicknames

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:09.940
- but he was soon to demonstrate the audacious
side of his military personality.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:15.400
Lee initiated the Seven Day’s Campaign on
June 25th, 1862, and he had soon pushed the

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.219
Union army from the outskirts of Richmond.

00:07:17.219 --> 00:07:21.771
By August 29th, he and his army were positioned
to do battle at Manassas, Virginia, where

00:07:21.771 --> 00:07:25.250
he defeated the Union army for a second time
before invading Maryland.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:29.780
This new commander of the Confederate army
had completely changed the military situation

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:31.509
in only a few weeks.

00:07:31.509 --> 00:07:35.879
In June, the entire Union army had been poised
to attack Richmond, but by September, Lee

00:07:35.879 --> 00:07:38.849
was in a position from which he could threaten
Washington, D.C.

00:07:38.849 --> 00:07:43.030
Lee’s army fought to a stalemate at the
Battle of Antietam on September 17th and withdrew

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.310
to the southern side of the Potomac.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:49.629
In December, Lee was able to conclude the
1862 campaign with a major victory over Ambrose

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:54.699
Burnside's Union forces at Fredericksburg,
Virginia sending the Northern army reeling

00:07:54.699 --> 00:07:56.699
back to bases near Washington.

00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:01.150
Lee and his hard-marching subordinate, ‘Stonewall’
Jackson, were able to defeat a larger Union

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:06.210
army under Joseph Hooker at Chancellorsville
in the first campaign of 1863, but the victory

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:07.290
was costly.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:11.280
Jackson was fatally wounded by his own men
while conducting a personal reconnaissance

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:12.280
of the enemy's lines.

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:17.449
This was a common feature of good Confederate
commanders; they would conduct their own reconnaissance

00:08:17.449 --> 00:08:19.370
during lulls in the battle in which they were
engaged.

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:23.960
Viewed as unnecessary bravado by some historians,
this was a key to the successful operations

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:28.939
of the Confederate commanders, and Lee himself
frequently undertook these hazardous operations.

00:08:28.939 --> 00:08:33.260
He returned from a personal battlefield reconnaissance
at Second Manassa s with a mark on his face

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.950
where a sharpshooter’s bullet had grazed
him.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.519
Lee’s immediate reaction to the death of
his most trusted officer was that it was God’s

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:42.750
will and that the Lord would raise someone
else up to take Jackson’s place.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.740
In that hope, however, he was mistaken.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:47.519
Lee would just have to do the best he could
by himself.

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:51.420
At Chancellorsville, Lee cemented his reputation
as a brilliant battlefield commander.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:54.959
It was a battle that seemingly, on paper,
he could not win.

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:58.700
Outnumbered two to one, he still divided his
army in half in the face of the enemy.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:03.600
He won the battle in spectacular fashion and
it was, undoubtedly, his most famous victory.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.040
The victory at Chancellorsville cleared the
way for another Confederate invasion of the

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.040
north.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.370
Confusion within the Union army combined with
the losses of the earlier battle gave Lee

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:16.490
the opportunity he longed for; a chance to
destroy the Union army in a classic Napoleonic

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.240
battle that would end the Civil War.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:23.440
This was a high-risk operation, but Lee placed
great faith in the ability of his Army of

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:24.663
Northern Virginia, for it had done the nearly
impossible before.

00:09:24.663 --> 00:09:25.663
Gettysburg

00:09:25.663 --> 00:09:29.339
At Gettysburg he would attempt to do it again.

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:43.250
In June of 1863, Lee boldly moved his army
of nearly 60,000 men into Union territory.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:47.440
His plan was to destroy as many military posts
as he could in Maryland and Pennsylvania as

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.370
possible while the Union army was forced to
defend Washington, D.C.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:54.150
A key target was Camp Curtin, just outside
of Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.790
This was the largest military supply depot
in the north.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:01.120
Lee’s main aim was not to defeat the Union
army militarily, but to bring the war to an

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.160
end politically.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.380
He believed that a massive defeat in the north
would encourage people to lose faith in the

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.000
war and demand it’s end.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.350
However, as the Confederate forces moved north,
a skirmish erupted in Gettysburg, Pennsylvania

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:13.980
between opposing cavalry units.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:18.420
Lee got word of the skirmish and was startled
to be informed that a major Union force was

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.019
closing in on Gettysburg.

00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:24.880
Instead of sending a cavalry reconnaissance
force to confirm the report, he sent his entire

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.310
army to mobilize.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:30.130
This was a colossal mistake.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:34.430
Lee ordered all of his forces to converge
at Cashtown, a small village seven miles from

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:35.430
Gettysburg.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:39.950
As the 60,000 Confederates poured in, around
3,000 union soldiers took position on McPherson

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:40.950
Ridge.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:44.600
These men tried to hold off the onslaught
of Rebel soldiers until help arrived.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.649
But reinforcements were miles away toward
Washington, D.C.

00:10:47.649 --> 00:10:51.769
The Union soldiers were forced to withdraw
southeast onto Cemetery Ridge, a range of

00:10:51.769 --> 00:10:55.709
hills on the outskirts of Gettysburg that
forms the shape of a fish-hook.

00:10:55.709 --> 00:10:59.260
This position provided an extremely strong
defensive advantage.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.610
Lee immediately saw the danger in the strength
of the Union position, but because of his

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:06.210
numerical superiority he believed that the
enemy was vulnerable.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:10.740
But Union forces soon arrived and strengthened
to defensive position.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:14.930
Though it was nowhere near Lee’s best campaign,
much of what happened at Gettysburg was still

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:16.850
remarkable from a southern perspective.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.110
Lee was outnumbered and without much of his
cavalry.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:24.160
The cavalry was used largely for reconnaissance,
to find the enemy and establish his size and

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.339
strength, but Lee’s cavalry regiments were
in the process of raiding around the entire

00:11:28.339 --> 00:11:33.440
Union army rather than gathering information
and screening the flanks of Lee’s army.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:37.730
Lee was also without his most trusted officer,
General Thomas ‘Stonewall’ Jackson, who

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:40.470
had been killed at Chancellorsville six weeks
earlier.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:44.190
Still, Lee failed adjust his strategy to the
situation on the ground.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:48.060
He refused to retreat, even when the situation
was clearly hopeless.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:52.180
After three days of terrible fighting and
unprecedented losses, the Confederates were

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:56.810
forced to withdraw from the field and make
a hasty retreat from Northern territory.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.880
As the survivors of the disastrous attack
that history remembers as Pickett’s charge

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.180
came fleeing back to the Southern position,
the General rode among the men trying to console

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.959
them and admitting that it was all his fault.

00:12:07.959 --> 00:12:12.209
In later reflection, Lee admitted that he
had simply asked too much of his men at Gettysburg.

00:12:12.209 --> 00:12:13.209
He wrote . . .

00:12:13.209 --> 00:12:17.550
No blame can be attached to the army for its
failure to accomplish what was projected by

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:18.550
me.

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:20.130
I am alone to blame.

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:22.920
Gettysburg was Lee’s greatest wartime disappointment.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:27.170
In its wake, he tendered his resignation to
Confederate President Jefferson Davis, writing,

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:28.180
Mr. President,

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:31.730
No one is more than myself of my inability
for the duties of my position.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:34.190
I cannot even accomplish what I myself desire.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:36.500
How can I fulfil the expectations of others.

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:40.770
In addition, I sensibly feel the growing failure
of my bodily strength.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.209
This was no idle self pitying.

00:12:42.209 --> 00:12:45.920
The Civil War, and in particular the battle
of Gettysburg, broke Lee physically.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.490
There is good evidence that, just before the
battle, he actually had a heart attack.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:54.440
Still, Davis refused to accept his resignation,
knowing that the man was irreplaceable.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:58.029
Following Gettysburg, Lee retreated across
the Potomac, never to return to the north

00:12:58.029 --> 00:12:59.029
again.

00:12:59.029 --> 00:13:02.170
The following Spring, a new Yankee general
came to the field to fight Lee.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.130
His name was Ulysses Simpson Grant.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:08.660
Finally the North had found a worthy contender
for the Great confederate general.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:11.899
Unlike many of his predecessors, Grant was
not covered by Lee.

00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:15.500
Both men under-estimated each other, soon
discovering that their opponent was different

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.010
from anyone else they had ever been up against.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:22.250
Immediately upon taking command, Grant issued
the following command to General Meade, commander

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:23.779
of the Army of the Potomac . . .

00:13:23.779 --> 00:13:26.030
Lee’s army will be your objective point.

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.949
Wherever Lee goes, there you will go also.

00:13:28.949 --> 00:13:34.061
Grant relentlessly pursued Lee from the Wilderness
to Spotsylvania and then to Cold Harbor, where

00:13:34.061 --> 00:13:36.000
a terrible slaughter took place.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.190
Throughout the bloody campaign and despite
heavy losses, Lee gallantly led his army.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:44.790
On May 6th, 1864, as his line was wavering,
he himself rode to the front and demanded

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:47.970
that his men stand their ground under very
dangerous circumstances.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:52.570
After June, 1864, Lee’s Army of Northern
Virginia had been pushed back into the defenses

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.209
of Richmond and Petersburg.

00:13:54.209 --> 00:13:58.320
Effectively Grant had taken him out of the
war, unable to maneuver and nowhere to go.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:03.660
All he could do now was to wait and react
as Grant, circling around him, acted.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:07.610
With his army besieged and pinned down, Lee
knew that the end was in sight.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:11.680
Lee’s anguish while being hemmed up at Petersburg
was heightened with the news that his home

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.680
in Arlington had been confiscated by the Union
government.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:18.910
Having been seized for unpaid taxes it was
quickly converted into a large soldier’s

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:19.910
cemetery.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:22.560
Orders were given for the graves to be placed
as close to the house as possible, so that

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.180
it could never be used again as a private
dwelling.

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:27.681
But the cruellest blow was yet to come.

00:14:27.681 --> 00:14:32.220
Through the winter of 1864, Lee’s army lacked
forage, rations and supplies.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.889
Somehow the men managed to retain their fighting
spirit.

00:14:34.889 --> 00:14:38.259
But even that was to disappear in April of
1865.

00:14:38.259 --> 00:14:42.610
With his men at near starvation point, Lee
led his bedraggled army to Appomattox Court

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:46.069
House in Virginia, following a last desperate
flight from Richmond.

00:14:46.069 --> 00:14:49.519
It was there that Lee surrendered his army
and ended the Civil War.

00:14:49.519 --> 00:14:53.389
The correspondence between Grant and Lee in
those closing hours of the war is enlightening.

00:14:53.389 --> 00:14:55.839
On April 7th, Grant sent the following message
to Lee . . .

00:14:55.839 --> 00:14:58.940
General,
The result of the last week must convince

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:01.950
you of the hopelessness of further resistance
in this struggle.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:06.140
I regard it as my duty to shift from myself
the responsibility of any further effusion

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:10.450
of blood by asking of you the surrender of
the army of Northern Virginia.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:12.829
Very Respectfully,
U.S. Grant.

00:15:12.829 --> 00:15:14.980
Lee’s reply came swiftly . . .

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.760
General Grant,
Though not entertaining the opinion you express

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.500
on the hopelessness of further resistance
on the part of the Army of Northern Virginia,

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:26.459
I reciprocate your desire to avoid useless
effusion of blood and, therefore, ask the

00:15:26.459 --> 00:15:29.350
terms you will offer in condition of its surrender.

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.440
Very Respectfully,
Robert E. Lee

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.370
On the morning of April 9th, Lee put on his
best uniform and went to the home of Wilmer

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:37.480
McLean where he met General Grant.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:41.860
The two men contrasted sharply - Lee six feet
high and with faultless form and dress and

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:45.990
Grant, five foot seven, mud spattered and
in a rumpled private’s uniform.

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:49.910
The terms of surrender were unexpectedly generous;
without demand for punishment, the Confederate

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:53.540
Army was allowed to go home on the provision
that they would never take up arms against

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:54.850
the Federal Government again.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:58.250
Many historians believe that it was a measure
of Grant’s respect for Lee that he handled

00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.600
the terms the way he did.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:03.600
As he exited the McLean House, Lee was cheered
by the Confederates and saluted by the Union

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:04.600
troops.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:14.930
The first officer of the Union to remove his
hat and salute Lee was U.S. Grant.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:18.990
After the War

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:22.220
After the war, Lee became president of Washington
College in Lexington, Virginia.

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:24.759
During those years, he also found time to
write his memoir.

00:16:24.759 --> 00:16:26.360
In it he stated the following . . .

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:27.970
I did only what my duty demanded.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:32.360
I could have taken no other course without
dishonor, and if it all were to be done over

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.610
again I should act in precisely the same manner.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.839
He dedicated his last years to education.

00:16:37.839 --> 00:16:41.079
On September 28th, 1870, he went to a school
meeting.

00:16:41.079 --> 00:16:42.910
On the way home, he caught a cold.

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:44.700
Fourteen days later he died.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.690
The huge funeral service at Lexington was
attended by thousands of Americans from both

00:16:48.690 --> 00:17:06.289
the North 

00:17:06.289 --> 00:17:13.659
and the South.

